/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  aminoBuffers01_tris_glycine -- the Tris and triethanolamine buffers at half neutralisation, and
  a glycine titration: 0.1 mol/kg glycine hydrochloride with 0.5, 1, 1.5 and 2 equivalents of NaOH,
  at 25 C.  Six beakers, the pH a RESULT of the charge balance, read against the evaluated pK's of
  Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231, Section 8 (p. 359-360).

  WHAT THIS CASE IS.  A STRUCTURAL ILLUSTRATION of the C373 phase 3 records (amine and amino-acid
  ladders, written by bin/curate/species_c373.py from data/curation/batches/species_c373).  It is
  NOT validated against a measured pH: no work in hand prints a pH for these solutions, so there
  are no `anchor` rows.  The master of each family is its conjugate ACID (HTris+, HTEOA+, H2Gly+),
  so the case feeds the hydrochloride (the chloride is the counter-ion) and NaOH is a titrant
  (`totalsBasis stoichiometric`); `pH solve;` closes the charge balance.  Tris: pK 8.072, dH
  47.45 kJ/mol; triethanolamine: pK 7.762, dH 33.6 (Section 7.68 p. 357, 7.66 p. 353); glycine:
  pK 2.351, 9.780 (Section 7.31 p. 281).

  WHAT TO LOOK FOR.  (this run's numbers, Davies, 25 C)
   1. Tris half-neutralised (0.025 + 0.025 mol/kg, I = 0.050): pH 8.157 = pK 8.072 + log10(1/0.8212),
      the cation's activity coefficient 0.8212 against 1 for the neutral base; triethanolamine
      the same: 7.848 = 7.762 + 0.086.  A buffer's pH is its pK only at zero ionic strength.
   2. Glycine at 1 equivalent of NaOH (the zwitterion): pH 6.075 against (pK1 + pK2)/2 = 6.066
      (the isoelectric point), the zwitterion 0.09995 of the 0.1 mol/kg, H2Gly+ and Gly- 2.4e-5
      and 2.5e-5.
   3. At 0.5 equivalent the first step is not a clean half: pH 2.526 against pK1 2.351, because the
      free H+ (3.8e-3 mol/kg) is not negligible beside the 0.1 mol/kg acid (H2Gly+ 0.0461,
      HGly 0.0539).  At 1.5 equivalents (Gly- 0.0499, HGly 0.0501) pH 9.660 = pK2 9.780 - 0.108,
      the anion's gamma 0.7596; at 2 equivalents the anion is 0.0976 of 0.1 and pH 11.259.
   4. No ion pairing with sodium for these anions and the HCl ion pair of the catalogue is the only
      one present (HClaq, 1e-4 or less): the distribution is complete only up to the reactions
      somebody wrote down.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    { name tris_half; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { HTris 0.05 mol/kg; Cl 0.05 mol/kg; Na 0.025 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( HTris Tris ); output { file tris_half.csv; } }
    { name triethanolamine_half; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { HTEOA 0.05 mol/kg; Cl 0.05 mol/kg; Na 0.025 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( HTEOA TEOA ); output { file triethanolamine_half.csv; } }
    { name glycine_Na0p050; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { H2Gly 0.10 mol/kg; Cl 0.10 mol/kg; Na 0.05 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H2Gly HGly Gly ); output { file glycine_Na0p050.csv; } }
    { name glycine_Na0p100; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { H2Gly 0.10 mol/kg; Cl 0.10 mol/kg; Na 0.10 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H2Gly HGly Gly ); output { file glycine_Na0p100.csv; } }
    { name glycine_Na0p150; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { H2Gly 0.10 mol/kg; Cl 0.10 mol/kg; Na 0.15 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H2Gly HGly Gly ); output { file glycine_Na0p150.csv; } }
    { name glycine_Na0p200; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { H2Gly 0.10 mol/kg; Cl 0.10 mol/kg; Na 0.20 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H2Gly HGly Gly ); output { file glycine_Na0p200.csv; } }
);
